## Runtime
<hr class="section-rule lvl-2">

!!! danger "Advanced topic"
    New users can safely skip this.  The runtime layer is built on the [supervisor]({{_}}/signals), so it only applies to interpreter-style (supervised) invocations.

This page covers the **core** runtime model: how a [supervised program]({{_}}/signals) treats the work it has *left to do* as a steerable **continuation**, plus the plugin-independent primitives `compose.mk` ships for transferring control over it.  The full, structured control-stack / CEK virtual machine[^1] built on top of these is the [`__vm__` module]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine).

### Continuations
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

!!! terminology "Continuation"
    The goals *after* the current target on the command line are the **continuation**[^2]: what's left to do.  `compose.mk` exposes that command line as `MAKE_CLI`, so a recipe can read, and re-shape, its own remaining goals.  This is the substrate everything else here is built on: a control transfer is just *"replace the continuation and re-enter."*

### Trampoline
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
<a name=flat-dispatch></a>

`mk.super.tramp` is the core transfer primitive: it re-dispatches the next continuation at **top level**, through the [supervisor]({{_}}/signals)'s trampoline loop (one of its [inlined bootloaders](#bootloader)).  It lives in the `mk.super.*` family because it needs that loop.

!!! info "Jumps are flat"
    Because every transfer re-enters at top level, iterative control flow runs at **constant process depth**; an unbounded loop never stacks up make processes.

Two related core primitives round out the picture; both are documented with the supervisor on the [Signals]({{_}}/signals) page:

* `mk.yield`: a process-oriented escape: it runs its target inline and then interrupts the make stack (so repeated use *nests*).  A general-purpose escape continuation.
* `mk.exit.code`: record-and-continue exit: stash an exact exit code out-of-band, then unwind *normally* so cleanup arms still run.

The trampoline's own state is a small **reflection surface**: seven read-only value-dunders exported on every dispatch hop.  They default empty, so an unsupervised read is safe, and the [`__vm__` snapshot]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine) reads them as its always-on layer.

* `__ip__`: the instruction pointer, the goal being run
* `__step__` / `__step_budget__`: the scheduler tick and its runaway guard
* `__alt__` / `__yielded__`: how the hop was reached (a backtrack alternative, or a transferred goal)
* `__posix_code__` / `__exit_code__`: raw wait status vs resolved exit code

All of these require an interpreter-style (supervised) invocation; `cmk run`, or an `mk.interpret` shebang, provides one.

### Root traceback handler
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

When a program fails **for real** (a genuinely unrouted nonzero exit, *not* an intentional control transfer), `compose.mk` presents the failure as a **typed fault** with a themed header, instead of leaking make's raw internal error.  So a bare missing goal reads like an interpreted program's error.  (Routing always wins; this handler is only the fallthrough.)

The built-in fault types cover the static, reproducible failures a program can hit:

| Fault | Raised when |
| --- | --- |
| **`RuleMissing`** | a goal names a target that does not exist (make's `No rule to make target`) |
| **`SyntaxError`** | the program is not legal make: e.g. a `missing separator`, or content that `commences before first target` |

The handler is *[faults-module]({{_}}/plugins/faults) compliant without depending on it*: with that plugin loaded it re-raises through `fault.throw`, so your own `fault/<Type>:` handlers can catch and route the fault; otherwise core just prints the themed default.  Only static (make-legal) faults are typed; a genuine runtime recipe failure is left untouched.

The same taxonomy backs [`cmk lint`]({{_}}/cmk/lint): its legality pass reports these types *before* you run a program, so a `SyntaxError` you would hit at runtime surfaces at lint-time too.

### Bootloader
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
<a name=bootloader></a>

The supervisor doesn't only *wrap* `make`; just before it runs your program it executes a small **bootloader**, and this is the single, header-free extension point for supervisor behavior.  Like the rest of this runtime layer the bootloader is a property of the [supervisor]({{_}}/signals), so it exists only for interpreter-style (supervised) invocations and never for `make -f compose.mk`.  It `source`s two kinds of script:

* **Inlined loaders, from `compose.mk` itself.**  Each builtin piece of supervisor behavior is a `define _mk.super.<name>` block in the source; the bootloader lifts that block's body out of the file and `source`s it in the supervisor's shell.  The core one is the trampoline dispatch loop ([flat dispatch](#flat-dispatch)) that powers the [virtual machine]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine).  Adding supervisor behavior is just another `define` plus one load line; no edit to the polyglot header.

* **A user-defined script, via `CMK_BOOTLOADER`.**  Point `CMK_BOOTLOADER` at exactly one file and the supervisor `source`s it **first**, at boot, before the trampoline runs the program.  Because it runs in the supervisor's own shell (not a subshell) it sees the pre-run state (the invocation's goals, the run id) and can **set up** the run (for example, install a TUI overlay), then reach the **end** of the run, where the resolved exit code is final, by registering an `EXIT` trap.  That makes it a clean place to bracket a whole run with setup/teardown.

!!! warning "A few rules for user bootloaders"
    * It must **not** trap `INT`/`TERM`; that would clobber the supervisor's own SIGINT handling.
    * If `CMK_BOOTLOADER` is set the file must exist (a missing file is fatal, and fails fast *before* any target runs).
    * Keep it **portable**: POSIX shell and tools that exist on Linux / OSX / Alpine-busybox.

Set `CMK_BOOTLOADER_DISABLED=1` to skip the bootloader entirely and run targets directly.

```bash {.cli_example}
$ CMK_BOOTLOADER=demos/user-bootloader.sh CMK_SUPERVISOR=1 ./compose.mk flux.ok
```
```bash {.cli_output}
user-bootloader: boot (goals=[flux.pre/flux.ok flux.ok flux.post/flux.ok])
Φ flux.ok // succeeding as requested!
user-bootloader: exit (code=0)
```

See the {{macros.repo_link('demos/user-bootloader.sh', name='user-bootloader demo', github=github)}} for a minimal example that proves it loaded at both ends of the run.

### Lifecycle & Boot Settings
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
<a name=lifecycle-boot-settings></a>

The [bootloader](#bootloader) above is the *mechanism*; the **boot settings** are its declarative surface, set in-source with the [`cmk_pragma` hints]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas) (or the matching environment).  A program's lifecycle is a set of list settings, **before -> main -> after**:

- `bootloaders` run *before* make, **in the supervisor's bash** (sourced, so they can register an `EXIT` trap or set up early state).  A bootloader is an inline named `define`: pure bash, single `$` (it is `source`d, not run by make), distinct from a make target.
- `cmk_pre` runs *before* the main pipeline too, but each substage is a **make target** (the "boot stage").  The framework runs them safely as a direct, unsupervised sub-make: the declarative equivalent of hand-writing a recursive `make` call in a bootloader.  A failing `cmk_pre` target aborts before main (the `cmk_post` handlers still run).
- `cmk_post` runs *after* the main pipeline (success **or** failure), each substage a **make target**.

`cmk_pre` and `cmk_post` are each one setting exposed across three surfaces with the same name, all accumulating:

* the pragma: `cmk_pre` / `cmk_post`
* the environment: `CMK_PRE` / `CMK_POST`
* a parse-time handle: `__cmk_pre__` / `__cmk_post__` (with `.append(<target>)`)

Two scalar toggles round out the boot settings: `hooks` (`off` disables the [pre/post target-rewriting](#pre-post-hooks)) and `bootloader_disabled`.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/pragma-boot.cmk', github=github)}}

These boot settings only take effect via `cmk run` (the path that owns both the source and the program's re-exec), and they are threaded into the canonical boot env (*not* the pragma namespace) because the bash header reads them before make exists.  The scalars are pragma-wins with a loud notice; the `bootloaders` list *appends* to any invoker-set `CMK_BOOTLOADER`.

!!! warning "`bootloaders` source code"
    A bootloader is sourced as shell at boot: powerful, and the same surface the shebang already exposes, but less visible.  An invoker can always block it with `CMK_BOOTLOADER_DISABLED=1`.  The **supervisor** itself (`CMK_SUPERVISOR`) is the one boot setting a pragma can never set, since it must come up before anything can be read.

### Virtual machine (module)
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
<a name=virtual-machine></a>
<a name=control-stack></a>

!!! warning "Non-core"
    The virtual machine is the opt-in [`virtual-machine.cmk` module]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine), not part of always-loaded core; the core runtime primitives above are enough for normal operation.  Load it via the [plugin system]({{_}}/plugins/overview) for the structured control stack.

The ergonomic, structured API over this substrate is a small CEK Machine[^1], roughly a "control stack" with:

* `peek`/`goto`/`call`
* `return`/`yield`/`fork`, 
* a reflective environment, 
* backtraces, reusable coroutines 

{% include "includes/cek-anim.md" %}


See also the [VM module docs]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine) for the operator table and worked push / goto / call-return / coroutine / generator examples.

### Pre & Post Hooks
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
<a name=pre-post-hooks></a>

When you run a program through an interpreter-style invocation (`cmk run`, or an `mk.interpret` shebang), you also **automatically get pre and post hooks for every target that runs via the command line.**  Here's a simple example to illustrate:

{{site.embed_demo('demos/hooking-targets.mk', github=github)}}

Running the code above works the way you'd expect, and we get pre and post effects with no explicit plumbing.  This is name-based with post-fixes as seen above, and **extensions can also add hooks for compose.mk builtins**.

Here's the output:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./demos/hooking-targets.mk main_target
```
```bash {.cli_output}
hello pre-hook
hello main
hello post-hook
```

Beyond readability, this enables a useful kind of code reuse: many targets can share the *same* pre/post-conditions.  A hook needs no explicit body, so declaring a target's `.pre` with a prerequisite reads as an annotation, like a python decorator, or lightweight "aspect-oriented" programming.

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

!!! warning "Hooks fire only from the command line"
    What does it mean that hooks work for "*every target used via command line*"?  **Hooks are only fired when the interpreter is used directly**; recursive `make` calls or similar will NOT execute them.  This makes the feature useful for standalone tools and for augmenting standard entrypoints like *clean/build/test*, and much less useful for libraries.

Hooks are enabled by default since they don't affect performance much, but it's possible they might conflict with existing naming conventions or similar.  To override this, you can set `CMK_DISABLE_HOOKS=1` in your environment, or in the shebang lines at the top of your script.  Always use `CMK_DISABLE_HOOKS=1` if you are making proxy-wrappers, as with the [`ls` clone]({{_}}/signals/#positional-arguments-yield) on the supervisor page.

### Related Config
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

The runtime layer is driven by a few supervisor/interpreter env-vars and reserved dunder names.  The canonical reference for both is the [Configuration]({{_}}/config) page.

> **Environment variables** ( [details]({{_}}/config#general-environment-variables) )
> {: .cmk-card }
>
> | Variable | Role |
> | --- | --- |
> | `CMK_SUPERVISOR` | Gate the [supervisor]({{_}}/signals) (interpreter-style invocations set it). |
> | `CMK_BOOTLOADER` / `CMK_BOOTLOADER_DISABLED` | The [bootloader](#bootloader) extension point (and the kill-switch). |
> | `CMK_DISABLE_HOOKS` | Disable the [pre/post hooks](#pre-post-hooks). |
> | `MAKE_CLI` | The live command line: the [continuation](#continuations) substrate. |
> | `MAKE_SUPER` | The supervisor's per-run id (scratch-file keying). |

<!-- separate adjacent card blockquotes (python-markdown merges them otherwise) -->

> **Dunder names** ( [details]({{_}}/config#dunder-names-__name__) )
> {: .cmk-card }
>
> | Name | Role |
> | --- | --- |
> | `__main__` | The default goal / entrypoint.  Runs on direct execution; demoted to a `<name>:` target on import ([Executable Modules]({{_}}/cmk/modules#executable-modules)). |
> | `__vm__` | The [virtual-machine](#virtual-machine) control stack (module). |
> | `__ip__` / `__step__` / `__posix_code__` / … | The scheduler's seven registers, exported on each dispatch hop as a read-only reflection surface (see [Flat dispatch](#flat-dispatch)). |
> | `__interpreter__` / `__interpreting__` | Invocation paths used for recursive `make` and forks. |

<!-- separate adjacent card blockquotes (python-markdown merges them otherwise) -->

> **Pragmas**: a program can also set compile-time settings in its `# cmk_pragma ::: {…} :::` header ( [details]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas) ); the few that shape the runtime:
> {: .cmk-card }
>
> | Pragma | Role |
> | --- | --- |
> | `repl` | Make the file a [REPL]({{_}}/embedded-tui) runtime execution mode (no import/boilerplate). |
> | `vm_hydrate` | Auto-receive the reflective [`__vm__`](#virtual-machine) env at every recipe entry (the recommended form). |
> | `vm_trace` | Inject live frame-tracing so the [virtual machine](#virtual-machine)'s frame-tree reflects the real recursive `make` call stack. |
> | `compiler_pre` / `compiler_post` | Append custom compile stages (an array, so it *accumulates*, e.g. a plugin lifting its own `vm_hydrate` stage). |

